recommendation:
Extend `onDeposit` and `onWithdraw` with explicit reward-amount parameters, mirroring the existing `onClaim` shape:

  interface IPhlimboHook {
      function onDeposit(
          address caller,
          address user,
          uint256 amount,
          uint256 sweptPhUSDAmount,
          uint256 sweptStableAmount
      ) external;
      function onWithdraw(
          address caller,
          address user,
          uint256 amount,
          uint256 sweptPhUSDAmount,
          uint256 sweptStableAmount
      ) external;
      function onClaim(...);  // unchanged
  }

Alternatively, emit a dedicated event from PhlimboV2 (e.g., `AutoClaimedDuringDeposit(user, caller, phUSDAmt, stableAmt)`) at the moment of auto-claim, and update hook integrators to subscribe to it. The interface extension is cleaner because the hook can react inline (e.g., revert if a sweep is unauthorized, or modify the hook's own bookkeeping atomically).

Document the migrator-role auto-claim path in IPhlimboV2 and IPhlimboHook so integrators know to treat stake/withdraw as potential reward-sweep events.

codeSnippet:
// stake — auto-claim is hidden from the hook:
if (userDetails.amount > 0) {
    _claimRewards(user, msg.sender);   // sweeps pending phUSD + stable to msg.sender
}
// ... state updates ...
emit Staked(user, amount);
if (address(hook) != address(0)) {
    hook.onDeposit(msg.sender, user, amount);   // <-- only `amount` is surfaced
}

// withdraw — same issue:
_claimRewards(user, msg.sender);   // auto-claim sweep
// ... state updates ...
if (address(hook) != address(0)) {
    hook.onWithdraw(msg.sender, user, actualWithdrawAmount);   // <-- only principal
}

// claim — by contrast, surfaces both amounts:
hook.onClaim(msg.sender, user, pendingPhUSDAmount, pendingRewardAmount);
